WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.675
Every small change to my videos
meant editing again,

00:00:03.777 --> 00:00:05.962
rendering again, and recording again.

00:00:06.273 --> 00:00:11.140
I want every detail exactly right, so I'd
watch it back and find one more thing.

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:14.333
I wanted to update a video
the way I update a doc.

00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:17.363
So I built this. Here's my script.

00:00:17.685 --> 00:00:20.358
I press build,
and it makes the narrated video.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:23.563
Watch this ball step down on my count.

00:00:24.698 --> 00:00:27.301
One. Two, three.

00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:32.839
I wrote the words and drew the pictures,
and each picture waits for its word.

00:00:33.194 --> 00:00:37.875
You just watched DeckTalk.
You write a script and simple slides.

00:00:38.175 --> 00:00:42.879
It reads your script aloud, and you
pick the word each picture waits for.

00:00:43.318 --> 00:00:48.800
Change the script, and the video follows.
Later, I'll add one word to this video.

00:00:49.552 --> 00:00:52.710
But first, here is a lesson
I made with DeckTalk,

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.335
about what that ball has to do with AI.

00:00:56.632 --> 00:01:00.419
How does AI learn?
From examples, not rules people write.

00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:03.310
Learning from examples
is called machine learning.

00:01:03.343 --> 00:01:06.074
An AI model is like a box of knobs.

00:01:06.129 --> 00:01:10.474
It guesses, and a meter shows its error,
how wrong the guess was.

00:01:10.866 --> 00:01:12.622
Learning nudges the knobs.

00:01:12.992 --> 00:01:16.674
It guesses again, and now it's right,
with a lower error.

00:01:17.019 --> 00:01:21.097
Then a new example,
and a smaller nudge fixes it.

00:01:21.385 --> 00:01:24.592
Example after example,
the model gets better.

00:01:25.599 --> 00:01:27.750
But which way should each knob turn?

00:01:27.875 --> 00:01:30.673
In a big model,
testing knobs one at a time

00:01:30.673 --> 00:01:33.335
would take billions
of tries for every step.

00:01:33.401 --> 00:01:37.467
Instead, one round of math gives
a direction for every knob at once.

00:01:38.325 --> 00:01:41.832
Picture two knobs.
The floor is every way to set them.

00:01:41.888 --> 00:01:45.629
The height is the error.
For this lesson it is a bowl.

00:01:47.484 --> 00:01:50.192
The ball is where the knobs
are set right now.

00:01:50.502 --> 00:01:54.174
It can't see the whole bowl,
only the slope where it stands.

00:01:54.369 --> 00:02:00.083
So it steps downhill, again and again, and
the steps shrink as the ground flattens.

00:02:00.371 --> 00:02:04.936
That is gradient descent.
It's what the ball did on my count.

00:02:05.874 --> 00:02:09.162
One setting makes every
step longer or shorter.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:14.886
Too long, and it bounces past the bottom.
Too short, and it is very slow.

00:02:16.022 --> 00:02:19.170
Zoom out, and the ground
can have many valleys.

00:02:19.585 --> 00:02:22.734
The ball finds a low one,
not always the lowest.

00:02:24.496 --> 00:02:27.611
Today's AI is trained
with a version of this.

00:02:27.991 --> 00:02:31.465
Inside, the knobs are grids of numbers,
called tensors.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:38.269
Chips like GPUs and TPUs multiply grids,
row times column, all at once.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.495
For a big model, the chips do

00:02:40.495 --> 00:02:44.248
billions of billions of small
calculations for every step.

00:02:44.791 --> 00:02:47.871
The large language models
behind AI chat apps

00:02:47.995 --> 00:02:50.517
split the work across thousands of chips.

00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:55.083
By hand, everyone on Earth
would need years for one step.

00:02:55.347 --> 00:02:57.068
The chips take seconds.

00:02:57.937 --> 00:03:01.098
That's the lesson.
Now let's step out of it.

00:03:01.675 --> 00:03:07.030
Everything you just watched is one video,
in five parts, made from one script.

00:03:07.446 --> 00:03:08.725
Remember my count?

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.249
Say I want one more step.

00:03:11.537 --> 00:03:16.265
I add one word, four,
and tell a new box to wait for it.

00:03:16.854 --> 00:03:21.129
I press build.
Only part one is read aloud again.

00:03:21.556 --> 00:03:25.483
The rest keep their voice.
Here is the new start.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:32.220
Watch this ball step down on my count.
One. Two, three, four.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.690
One more count, one more box.

00:03:34.908 --> 00:03:39.229
Every picture in the count moved to stay
on its word, and each one is checked.

00:03:39.354 --> 00:03:42.283
I didn't touch a timeline,
or record a new take.

00:03:42.431 --> 00:03:45.963
So I can keep changing this
video until it's exactly right.

00:03:46.088 --> 00:03:47.403
Now let's step back in.

00:03:48.017 --> 00:03:50.527
Update your video the way
you update a doc.

00:03:51.024 --> 00:03:54.881
DeckTalk. Open source,
narrated presentations, cut to the word.

00:03:55.041 --> 00:03:57.366
Make your own at decktalk dot app.
